!!id:"https://tson.io/test-suite/class2/schema/valid/an-enum-declares-which-kind-of-enumeration-it-is-expected.tn"
!!schema:"https://tson.io/test-suite/schemas/schema-sidecar.tn"
{
  spec: "[TSON-SCHEMA] §7.4, [TSON-SCHEMA] §9"
  description: """
    An enum's members are labels drawn from a naming vocabulary, its label type, and the constructor
    says which: `enum` is `enum_of<identifier>` and `text_enum` is `enum_of<text>`. `status` is a
    vocabulary: its members are names, so they stay a named scope for [TSON-DATA] §8.2's hygiene.
    `activity` is a value set of arbitrary text: two strings a document may carry, neither of them a
    name and neither writable without quotes.

    What separates them is the declaration, never the shape of the members -- a resolver inferring
    the kind from how the members happen to be spelled would switch a spoofing check on and off by
    accident. Each instance records the constructor it applied as its `source`, and the label type is
    that constructor's own `enum_of<T>` application.
    """
  meta: "meta.tn"
  import: ["core.tn"]
  valid: {
    resolved: """
      !schema {
        status => !type_definition {
          source: enum
          body: !enum { members: [OPEN ACTIVE DONE] }
        }

        activity => !type_definition {
          source: text_enum
          body: !text_enum { members: ["sedentary" "lightly active"] }
        }
      }
      """
  }
}
